{"tb-E302-1":{"number":"1","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following is the unit of illuminance?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A lm </b></i></p><p><i><b>B lm s</b></i><i><b><sup>−1</sup></b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C lm W</b></i><i><b><sup>−1</sup></b></i><i><b> </b></i></p><p><i><b>D lm m</b></i><i><b><sup>−2</sup></b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":3},"id":"tb-E302-1","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-2":{"number":"2","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following statements about an </b></i><i><b>incandescent lamp is correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) Visible light is produced by the electron </b></i><i><b>transitions occurring in its filament.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) Its luminous flux is generally lower than that </b></i><i><b>of a compact fluorescent lamp.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) Its efficacy is generally higher than that of </b></i><i><b>a compact fluorescent lamp.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (2) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":10},"id":"tb-E302-2","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-3":{"number":"3","question":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/a1f3a0289ba75bae3ffd3aa7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:312.4px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Light bulbs </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>R </b></i><i><b>emit monochromatic </b></i><i><b>light of the same power (Fig a). Arrange the </b></i><i><b>illuminance at </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Z </b></i><i><b>in ascending order.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Z</b></i><i><b> </b></i></p><p><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Z</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Z</b></i><i><b> </b></i></p><p><i><b>D </b></i><i><b>Z</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>X</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":20},"id":"tb-E302-3","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-4":{"number":"4","question":"<p><i><b>Lamp </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>has a higher efficacy than lamp </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>. The </b></i><i><b>two lamps have the same input power. Which of </b></i><i><b>the following statements must be correct? </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) Lamp </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>looks brighter than lamp </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) The cost of electricity in using lamp </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>is </b></i><i><b>lower than lamp </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) The human eye is more sensitive to the light </b></i><i><b>emitted by lamp </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>than that by lamp </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (2) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (1), (2) and (3)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":28},"id":"tb-E302-4","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-5":{"number":"5","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following statements about the </b></i><i><b>luminous flux of a point source of light is/are </b></i><i><b>correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) It is inversely proportional to the square of </b></i><i><b>the distance from the source.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) Its unit is lm.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) It is the total power of visible light emitted by </b></i><i><b>the source.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B (3) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D (1) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":38},"id":"tb-E302-5","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-6":{"number":"6","question":"<p><i><b>Plane surfaces </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>are put under a parallel </b></i><i><b>light beam in different ways as shown. The area </b></i><i><b>of </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is larger than that of </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b>. In which case(s) is/</b></i> <i><b>are the illuminance on the surface the largest?</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/9a0a7f071f2dd93166285e17.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:321.2px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (3) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B (4) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D (2) and (4) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p><b>  </b>Since <i>E</i> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ cos θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, </p><p>  <i>θ</i> ↑ ⇒ cos <i>θ</i> ↓ ⇒ <i>E</i> ↓</p><p><i>  </i>∴ The illuminance on the surface in (2) is smaller than that in (1), and that in (4) is smaller than that in (3).</p><p>  The illuminance is related to the area of the parallel light beam, not the areas of <i>P</i> and <i>Q</i>.</p><p>  ∴ The illuminance on the surface in (1) is the same as that in (3).</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":48},"id":"tb-E302-6","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-7":{"number":"7","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following statements about an LED </b></i><i><b>is/are correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) It emits EM waves of a broad range of </b></i><i><b>frequencies.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) It can be operated by d.c. only.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) The frequencies of the EM waves emitted </b></i><i><b>depend on the voltage across it.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (2) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (1), (2) and (3)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":61},"id":"tb-E302-7","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-8":{"number":"8","question":"<p><i><b>A photo on a wall faces a point source of light </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>as shown (Fig b). Incident light from </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>makes an </b></i><i><b>angle of 60° with the normal at point </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>of the </b></i><i><b>photo. The distance between </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>is </b></i><i><b>r </b></i><i><b>and the </b></i><i><b>illuminance at </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>due to </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>is </b></i><i><b>x</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/c4dd1399fceefcac7823b975.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:293.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>If the distance between </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>is increased to 2</b></i><i><b>r </b></i><i><b>and light from </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>still hits </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>at 60°, what will the </b></i><i><b>illuminance at </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>due to </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>become?</b></i></p><p>A  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>B  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>C  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>D  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Illuminance =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φcos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>∝<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> <br/>  (Φ and <i>θ</i> constant)</p><p>  ∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>÷<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>  <i>E</i><sub>2</sub>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>× <i>E</i><sub>1</sub></p><p>    = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(2R)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>× <i>x</i></p><p>    = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math><i>x</i></p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":71},"id":"tb-E302-8","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-9":{"number":"9","question":"<p><i><b>Lamps </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>have the same luminous flux. </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>emits monochromatic red light while </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>emits </b></i><i><b>monochromatic green light. Which of the </b></i><i><b>following statements must be correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) The efficacy of </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is lower than that of </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) The input power of </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is larger than that of </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) The useful output power of </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is larger than </b></i><i><b>that of </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B (3) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D (2) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":85},"id":"tb-E302-9","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-10":{"number":"10","question":"<p><i><b>A CFL, which can be considered as a point source, </b></i><i><b>is located at a distance </b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>from a plane surface </b></i><i><b>(Fig c). The illuminance at point </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>on the </b></i><i><b>surface is </b></i><i><b>E</b></i><i><b>. If the illuminance at </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>is 2</b></i><i><b>E</b></i><i><b>, what </b></i><i><b>is </b></i><i><b>θ</b></i><i><b>?</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/cc566c930a14d20b3cb38b35.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:291.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>15.2°</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>35.9°</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 54.1°</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D 74.8° </b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Consider a point which is <i>s</i> away from the CFL.</p><p>  Since <i>s</i> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p>  <i>E</i>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φcos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><i>  </i><i>  </i>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ cos α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>    = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>    ∝ cos<sup>3</sup> <i>α</i></p><p><i>  </i>∴  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>    <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(90° − θ)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(90° − 40°)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>    cos (90° – <i>θ</i>)  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mroot><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mroot></mrow></math>cos 50°</p><p>    <i>θ</i>  = 54.1°</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":95},"id":"tb-E302-10","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-11":{"number":"11","question":"<p><i><b>A torch of luminous flux 15 lm emits a parallel </b></i><i><b>light beam. A plane surface </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>of area 25 cm</b></i><i><b><sup>2</sup></b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>blocks the light beam and a circular region of </b></i><i><b>diameter 6 cm is illuminated (Fig d). If surface </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>is removed and another plane surface </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>of </b></i><i><b>area 2 cm</b></i><i><b><sup>2</sup></b></i><i><b> is placed to block part of the light </b></i><i><b>beam (Fig e), what is the illuminance at point </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>on surface </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>?</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/ae7f6b935b0fb5995256e11e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:300.7px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/f3b62fa49475d00241a0987a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:299.2px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>119 lx</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>5310 lx</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 6000 lx</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D 75 000 lx </b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Illuminance = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><b>  </b><b>  </b><b>  </b>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>15</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>π × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(0.03)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 5310 lx</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":114},"id":"tb-E302-11","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-15":{"number":"15","question":"<p><i><b>Tina wants to buy a lamp. The salesman tells </b></i><i><b>her that an 8-W LED lamp appears as bright as </b></i><i><b>a 40-W incandescent lamp (Fig j). The efficacy </b></i><i><b>of the LED lamp is 60 lm W</b></i><i><b><sup>−1</sup></b></i><i><b>.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/87b38ffcbf7b4b3c00a4f468.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:319.7px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) What is the luminous flux of the LED lamp?</b></i> <i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) What is the efficacy of the incandescent </b></i><i><b>lamp? </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Tina uses the lamp for 5 hours a day on </b></i><i><b>average. The cost of electricity is $1.1 per </b></i><i><b>kW h. How much electricity cost did Tina </b></i><i><b>saved per year if she uses the LED lamp? </b></i><i><b>Take 1 year = 365 days. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(d) Besides energy efficiency, state another </b></i><i><b>advantage of an LED lamp over an </b></i><i><b>incandescent lamp. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  (a)</b>  Luminous flux</p><p>    = efficacy × input power  1M</p><p>    = 60 × 8</p><p>    = 480 lm  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  Efficacy  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>luminous flux</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>input power</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><b>  </b><b>  </b><b>  </b>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>480</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>40</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 12 lm W<sup>−</sup><sup>1</sup>  1A</p><p><b>  (c)</b>  Difference in cost of electricity</p><p>    = 1.1 ×<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>40−8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1000</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>× 5 × 365</p><p>    = $64.2  1A</p><p>  <b>(d)</b>  It has a longer lifetime.  1A</p><p>    (Or other reasonable answers)</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":126},"id":"tb-E302-15","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-16":{"number":"16","question":"<p><i><b>Both the Earth and Mars are planets in the solar </b></i><i><b>system. The distance of Mars from the Sun is </b></i><i><b>about 1.5 times the distance of the Earth from </b></i><i><b>the Sun (Fig k). At a certain time, sunlight falls </b></i><i><b>perpendicularly on a city on the Earth, making </b></i><i><b>the illuminance at the city to be 1.3 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>5</sup></b></i><i><b> lx. </b></i><i><b>Assume the sunlight is not blocked when </b></i><i><b>travelling to the planets.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/0ff20c191a8f0d9e86955f33.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:328.5px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Estimate the illuminance at a point on Mars </b></i><i><b>where sunlight falls perpendicularly on it. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) The Earth is 1.5 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>11</sup></b></i><i><b> m away from the </b></i><i><b>Sun. Estimate the luminous flux of the Sun. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  Illuminance =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>∝<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> (Φ constant)</p><p>    ∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>÷<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>  <i>E</i><sub>2</sub>  =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>× <i>E</i><sub>1</sub></p><p>    =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(1.5r</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.3 × 10<sup>5</sup></p><p>    = 5.78 × 10<sup>4</sup> lx</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  Φ = <i>AE</i></p><p>    = 4π<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> × <i>E</i></p><p><i>  </i><i>  </i>= 4π × (1.5 × 10<sup>11</sup>)<sup>2</sup> × 1.3 × 10<sup>5</sup></p><p>    = 3.68 × 10<sup>28</sup> lm</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":146},"id":"tb-E302-16","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-17":{"number":"17","question":"<p><i><b>(a) Describe, in terms of electron transitions, </b></i><i><b>the working principle of an incandescent </b></i><i><b>lamp. </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Hence explain why the efficacy of the lamp </b></i><i><b>is low. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) State an advantage and a disadvantage of an </b></i><i><b>incandescent lamp when compared with a </b></i><i><b>CFL. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  (a)</b>  When a voltage is applied across the filament, the electric current heats up the filament.  1A</p><p>    As a result, electrons in the filament are excited to different higher levels.  1A</p><p>    When the electrons drop to many different lower energy levels, they release EM waves of a broad range of frequencies.  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  Only a small potion of the EM waves emitted is visible.  1A</p><p><b>  (c)</b>  Advantage: It does not contain mercury.  1A</p><p>    Disadvantage: Its lifetime is short.  1A</p><p>    (Or other reasonable answers)</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":161},"id":"tb-E302-17","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-18":{"number":"18","question":"<p><i><b>Consider point </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>on a plane surface. A point light </b></i><i><b>source has an input power of 60 W and is 2 m </b></i><i><b>away from </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>as shown (Fig l). The illuminance </b></i><i><b>at </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>is 50 lx.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/00793a6e4655ba0c00439b2f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:208.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) What is meant by illuminance? </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Find the luminous flux of the point light  </b></i><i><b>source. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Find the efficacy of the point light source. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  (a)</b>  Illuminance on a surface is the luminous flux falling on a surface per unit area.<br/>  1A</p><p><b>  (b)</b>  By <i>E</i> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ cos θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,  1M</p><p>    luminous flux = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> × 50</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos(90° − 60°)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 2902 lm</p><p>      ≈ 2900 lm  1A</p><p>  <b>(c)</b>  Efficacy = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>luminous flux</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>input power</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>      = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2902</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>60</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 48.4 lm W<sup>−</sup><sup>1</sup>  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":173},"id":"tb-E302-18","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-19":{"number":"19","question":"<p><i><b>Figure m shows the energy label of a 20-W CFL.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/3aa9a45647391445c9eda100.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:203.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) What is the luminous flux of the lamp? </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Bonnie installs the CFL on the ceiling of her </b></i><i><b>room (Fig n). Assume the lamp is a point </b></i><i><b>source. </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is a point on the desk.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/85101ebdfa27f5421b334901.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:287.5px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(i) Estimate the illuminance at </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b>. (2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(ii) Is the actual value of the illuminance at </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b> larger than, equal to or smaller than, </b></i><i><b>the value found in (i)? Explain briefly. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) The CFL has a special coating on its glass </b></i><i><b>tube. Explain its function. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(d) An incandescent lamp of the same </b></i><i><b>brightness consumes a much higher power. </b></i><i><b>Explain why. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b></b>  <b>(a)</b>  Luminous flux  = 70 × 20  1M</p><p>      = 1400 lm  1A</p><p>  <b>(</b><b>b</b><b>)</b><b>  (</b><b>i</b><b>)</b><b>  </b>Illuminance at <i>P</i></p><p>      = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ cos θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>      = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1400 cos(90° − 50°)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 26.3 lx  1A</p><p>    <b>(ii)</b>  The actual value is larger  1A</p><p>  <b>  </b><b>  </b>since light is reflected by the walls and ceiling onto the desk.  1A</p><p><b>  </b><b>(</b><b>c</b><b>)</b>  The coating absorbs the ultra-violet radiation  1A</p><p>    and emits visible light.  1A</p><p>  <b>(d)</b>  The EM waves emitted are mainly infra-red radiation,  1A</p><p>    which cannot be used for illumination.<br/>  1A</p><p>Editorial correction: part (b)(i) uses the luminous flux of 1400 lm found in part (a). The supplied solution used 1750 lm and obtained 32.9 lx; the corrected illuminance is 26.3 lx.</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":189},"id":"tb-E302-19","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-20":{"number":"20","question":"<p><i><b>Mike is going to buy a lamp with a luminous </b></i><i><b>flux greater than 1100 lm. He finds the following </b></i><i><b>lamps in a shop (Table a).</b></i></p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><b>Lamp</b></p></td><td><p><b>Power rating</b></p></td><td><p><b>Efficacy</b></p></td><td><p><b>Price</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>P</b></p></td><td><p><b>15 W</b></p></td><td><p><b>90 lm W⁻¹</b></p></td><td><p><b>$50</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Q</b></p></td><td><p><b>60 W</b></p></td><td><p><b>17 lm W⁻¹</b></p></td><td><p><b>$30</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>R</b></p></td><td><p><b>100 W</b></p></td><td><p><b>12 lm W⁻¹</b></p></td><td><p><b>$10</b></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Table a</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Show that lamp </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>does not fulfil Mike’s </b></i><i><b>requirement. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Consider lamps </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>R</b></i><i><b>. The cost of </b></i><i><b>electricity is $1.1 per kW h. Find the number </b></i><i><b>of days it takes for the difference in cost of </b></i><i><b>electricity to compensate for the difference </b></i><i><b>in price between the lamps if the lamps are </b></i><i><b>used for 8 hours a day. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Among lamps </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>R</b></i><i><b>, one is a compact </b></i><i><b>fluorescent lamp (CFL) and the other is </b></i><i><b>an incandescent lamp. </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(i) Describe how visible light is produced by </b></i><i><b>a CFL. </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(ii) Explain which lamp is the CFL. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(d) Mike buys the CFL and installs it in his </b></i><i><b>room (Fig o). Assume the lamp is a point </b></i><i><b>source.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/58fb60a6ce9bd746a959c918.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:296.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>is a point on the surface of the mattress. </b></i><i><b>The line connecting the lamp and </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>makes </b></i><i><b>an angle of 30° with the normal at </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>. Find </b></i><i><b>the illuminance at </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  Luminous flux of <i>Q</i></p><p>    = efficacy × input power</p><p>    = 17 × 60</p><p>    = 1020 lm (&lt; 1100 lm)  1A</p><p><b>  (b)</b>  Difference in cost of electricity per day</p><p>    = 1.1 ×<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>100 − 15</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1000</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>× 8 </p><p>    = $0.748</p><p>    ∴ 50 − 10 = 0.748<i>t</i>  1M</p><p><i>  t</i> = 53.5 days</p><p>    ∴ It takes 53.5 days.  1A</p><p>  <b>(</b><b>c</b><b>)</b><b>  (</b><b>i</b><b>)</b><b>  </b>A high voltage is applied across the electrodes of the lamp. The electric current heats up the tube and the mercury inside the tube vaporizes.  1A</p><p>      Electron transitions occur in the mercury atoms to emit mainly ultra-violet radiation.  1A</p><p>      The phosphor coating absorbs ultra-violet radiation and emits visible light.  1A</p><p>    <b>(ii)</b>  Lamp <i>P</i> is the compact fluorescent lamp  1A</p><p>      since it has a higher efficacy.  1A</p><p>  <b>(d)</b>  Illuminance = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ cos θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>      = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>15 </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> 90</mtext></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>30</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> 2.</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>      = 14.9 lx  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":213},"id":"tb-E302-20","chapter":"E302"},"tb-E302-21":{"number":"21","question":"<p><i><b>A student performs an experiment to estimate the </b></i><i><b>luminous flux of a CFL (Fig p).</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/0a7f2e3261bb261a6b4f469b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:335.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> The student uses a light meter to measure the </b></i><i><b>illuminance </b></i><i><b>E </b></i><i><b>at different distances </b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>from the </b></i><i><b>CFL and the results are as follows.</b></i></p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><b>d / m</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.0</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.1</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.2</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.3</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.4</b></p></td><td><p><b>1.5</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>E / lx</b></p></td><td><p><b>119</b></p></td><td><p><b>102</b></p></td><td><p><b>81</b></p></td><td><p><b>73</b></p></td><td><p><b>60</b></p></td><td><p><b>55</b></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Table b</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Plot </b></i><i><b>E </b></i><i><b>against a suitable quantity to obtain </b></i><i><b>a straight line graph which can demonstrate </b></i><i><b>the inverse-square law. </b></i><i><b>(4 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Estimate the luminous flux of the CFL from </b></i><i><b>the graph in (a). </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Suggest two ways to improve the accuracy of </b></i><i><b>this experiment. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  (a)</b>  </p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><i><b>d</b></i><b> / m</b></p></td><td><p>1.0</p></td><td><p>1.1</p></td><td><p>1.2</p></td><td><p>1.3</p></td><td><p>1.4</p></td><td><p>1.5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i><b>E</b></i><b> / lx</b></p></td><td><p>119</p></td><td><p>102</p></td><td><p>81</p></td><td><p>73</p></td><td><p>60</p></td><td><p>55</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math><b>/ m</b><b><sup>−</sup></b><b><sup>2</sup></b></p></td><td><p>1.0</p></td><td><p>0.83</p></td><td><p>0.69</p></td><td><p>0.59</p></td><td><p>0.51</p></td><td><p>0.44</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>    (Correct data)  1A</p><p><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/1cc77e2a2e4e33212c68bc00.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:315.7px\"/></p><p>    (Correct labelled axes with units)  1A</p><p>    (Correct data points)  1A</p><p>    (Straight line passing through origin)  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  By <i>E</i> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,  1M</p><p>  slope of graph  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>120 − 0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.0 − 0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>  = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Φ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>  Φ  = 1510 lm  1A</p><p>    The luminous flux is 1510 lm.</p><p>  <b>(c)</b>  Black out the laboratory.  1A</p><p>    Cover the bench with non-reflective material.  1A</p><p>    (Or other reasonable answers)</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"E302 Revision 2 Lighting.docx","sha256":"0b9d05b38fedcb61c33f719b22f14cf4790d41b2e377347ca40100b6211548b4","block":244},"id":"tb-E302-21","chapter":"E302"}}